-- Closed-loop execution: an order the venue reports that nobody authorised.
--
-- A trading desk lets a program place orders at a venue - an exchange
-- or a broker's platform - for power, gas, anything with a market. The
-- program is an agent: it reads prices, decides, and proposes. Nobody
-- on the desk trusts it with the venue's credentials, so it never holds
-- them. It proposes an order here; if the record admits the order, a
-- separate process, the executor, sends it to the venue and nothing
-- else. The executor holds the credential and never decides anything.
--
-- That arrangement prevents a great deal. The agent cannot trade an
-- instrument it was not given, or beyond its size, because the record
-- refuses the proposal before any order exists. But prevention has an
-- edge. Morpholog can refuse an unlawful order. It cannot see a second
-- credential somebody left in an old deployment, or a venue login a
-- person uses by hand, and an order placed that way is simply invisible
-- to a system that only governs its own record.
--
-- The venue, though, keeps its own record of every order it accepted.
-- This example closes the loop: the venue's report comes back in as
-- claims, admitted like any other, and a deterministic read compares
-- what the venue says happened with what this record authorised. Four
-- outcomes come out of that comparison. One matters more than the
-- rest: an order the venue reports that no admitted act authorised.
-- The record cannot have prevented it. It can expose it.
--
-- Three parties act on this record, each a login of its own. A trader
-- (the agent) proposes orders. A reporter brings the venue's reports in.
-- An operator appoints the other two and sets what they may do. The
-- executor is the fourth party and never proposes: it consumes admitted
-- orders from the record's outbox and sends each one to the venue under
-- the order's own identifier as the client reference, which is the key
-- the venue echoes back and the reconciliation joins on.

program closed_loop_execution

-- The operator is the trust root. Every grant below asks "is the
-- proposer the operator?", so a login that could merely SAY it is the
-- operator could hand itself anything. The runtime can bind an actor's
-- name to the database logins allowed to speak it: the first claim
-- arms a name, the second grants one login the right to it. Once a
-- name is armed, a proposal naming it from any other login is refused
-- before the body runs. The two claims are separate so that withdrawing
-- a login's last grant locks the name instead of returning it to the
-- open - the safe direction to fail in.
predicate Operator(operator: Subject)
predicate ActorAssertionRestricted(actor: Subject)
predicate ActorAssertionAuthority(actor: Subject, login_role: Subject)

-- A venue the desk trades at, and who may speak for its reports.
predicate VenueDeclared(venue: Subject)
predicate FeedOperator(reporter: Subject, venue: Subject)

-- A trader's mandate: which instrument, and the largest single order
-- allowed. This is a cap per order, not a position or exposure limit;
-- holding a running total under a limit is the trade-lifecycle
-- example's job, and it needs more state than this record keeps.
predicate TradingMandate(trader: Subject, instrument: Subject, max_qty: Decimal)
    unique by (trader, instrument)

-- An authorised order: the terms the record admitted, and the act that
-- admitted them is the only way it comes to exist. Append only: an
-- authorisation, once given, stays on the record whatever the venue
-- does with it afterwards.
predicate OrderAuthorised(order: Subject, venue: Subject, instrument: Subject,
                          side: Subject, qty: Decimal, price: Decimal)
    unique by (order)
    append only

-- What the venue says happened, as the venue said it. `order_ref` is
-- the client reference the venue echoes: the order's own identifier
-- when the executor placed it, and whatever the sender supplied
-- otherwise. `venue_order_id` is the venue's own identifier for the
-- order it accepted. The venue numbers its report messages, and the
-- uniqueness on (venue, sequence) is what makes ingestion safe to
-- repeat: a reporter that replays a message it already brought in is
-- refused by name, and never manufactures a second venue event.
--
-- There is deliberately NO rule here saying a report must name an
-- authorised order. That rule would make an unauthorised order
-- uncommittable in this record while leaving it done at the venue -
-- the record would hide exactly what it should expose. Evidence of a
-- bypass is admitted as evidence.
predicate VenueReport(report: Subject, venue: Subject, sequence: Decimal,
                      order_ref: Subject, venue_order_id: Subject,
                      instrument: Subject, side: Subject, qty: Decimal, price: Decimal)
    unique by (venue, sequence)
    append only

-- The one intent: an admitted order the executor must place. Its
-- arguments carry the order's identifier, and the executor sends that
-- identifier as the venue's client reference on every attempt. Delivery
-- is at least once - a crash between the venue accepting and the record
-- noting it means the order is sent again - and the reference is what
-- lets a venue that deduplicates act once.
intent PlaceOrder(order: Subject, venue: Subject, instrument: Subject,
                  side: Subject, qty: Decimal, price: Decimal)

-- The record's only rule about the orders it holds. The gate on
-- `place_order` already refuses a non-positive order; this says the
-- state cannot hold one however it got there, which is what a reader
-- of the record is promised.
invariant authorised_order_is_positive:
    OrderAuthorised(order, _, _, _, qty, price) implies 0 < qty and 0 < price

-- Putting the operator on the record, and closing the door behind it
-- in the same act. Arm first and grant later, and between the two
-- nobody could act as the operator at all, including to issue the
-- grant. The login named here is the only one that can act as the
-- operator afterwards; name the wrong one and the remedy is a fresh
-- deployment, which is the right way for a trust root to fail.
transformation appoint_operator(operator, login_role):
    require not Operator(_)
    admit Operator(operator)
    admit ActorAssertionRestricted(operator)
    admit ActorAssertionAuthority(operator, login_role)

-- Binding a name to the login that may speak it. The operator does this
-- for the trader and the reporter before granting either anything,
-- because authority given to a name anyone may speak is authority
-- given to everyone.
transformation enrol_login(principal, login_role):
    require Operator(actor)
    require not ActorAssertionAuthority(principal, login_role)
    admit ActorAssertionRestricted(principal)
    admit ActorAssertionAuthority(principal, login_role)

transformation declare_venue(venue):
    require Operator(actor)
    admit VenueDeclared(venue)

-- A mandate goes only to an armed name: the gate asks for the arming
-- claim, not for a particular login, so the operator enrols first.
transformation grant_mandate(trader, instrument, max_qty):
    require Operator(actor)
    require ActorAssertionRestricted(trader)
    require 0 < max_qty
    admit TradingMandate(trader, instrument, max_qty)

transformation grant_feed(reporter, venue):
    require Operator(actor)
    require VenueDeclared(venue)
    require ActorAssertionRestricted(reporter)
    admit FeedOperator(reporter, venue)

-- The agent's one act. The gate asks three things of the proposer: a
-- mandate for this instrument, a size within it, and a side that is
-- one of the two. The order's identifier is drawn fresh here - the
-- agent cannot choose it, so it cannot collide with or impersonate
-- another order's reference at the venue. Admitting the authorisation
-- and emitting the intent are one act: there is no admitted order
-- without an intent to place it, and no intent without the admitted
-- order it carries.
transformation place_order(venue, instrument, side, qty, price):
    require VenueDeclared(venue)
    require TradingMandate(actor, instrument, max_qty) and 0 < qty and qty <= max_qty
    require 0 < price
    require side = #buy or side = #sell
    let order = new Subject()
    admit OrderAuthorised(order, venue, instrument, side, qty, price)
    emit PlaceOrder(order, venue, instrument, side, qty, price)

-- The venue's report enters the record. The gate asks only whether the
-- proposer may speak for this venue; it does not ask whether the
-- report names an authorised order, for the reason given above.
transformation observe_venue_report(venue, sequence, order_ref, venue_order_id, instrument, side, qty, price):
    require FeedOperator(actor, venue)
    let report = new Subject()
    admit VenueReport(report, venue, sequence, order_ref, venue_order_id, instrument, side, qty, price)

-- The reconciliation: four reads over the two records, each a finding
-- per row, none of them stored. They are computed from the admitted
-- claims whenever asked, so the answer as of any committed history is
-- the same answer every time, and a receipt can be issued over it.

-- An authorised order the venue reports on every term: the same venue,
-- instrument, side, quantity and price under the order's own
-- reference. Agreement on the reference alone would not do - a venue
-- echoing the right reference against the wrong size would look
-- matched, which is the opposite of what this read is for.
predicate Matched(order: Subject, report: Subject, qty: Decimal)
derived Matched(order, report):
    over (OrderAuthorised(order, venue, instrument, side, qty, price)
          and VenueReport(report: report, venue: venue, order_ref: order,
                          instrument: instrument, side: side, qty: qty, price: price, ..))
    value qty = value OrderAuthorised(order: order, qty: _, ..)

-- A report whose reference names an authorised order, but on different
-- terms. The reference was honoured and the order was not: a bypass
-- shaped like an executor that sent the right reference with a size
-- of its own.
predicate Mismatched(report: Subject, order: Subject, reported_qty: Decimal, authorised_qty: Decimal)
derived Mismatched(report, order):
    over (VenueReport(report: report, venue: venue, order_ref: order,
                      instrument: instrument, side: side, qty: qty, price: price, ..)
          and OrderAuthorised(order, _, _, _, _, _)
          and not OrderAuthorised(order, venue, instrument, side, qty, price))
    value reported_qty = value VenueReport(report: report, qty: _, ..)
    value authorised_qty = value OrderAuthorised(order: order, qty: _, ..)

-- An authorised order the venue has not reported. There is no time
-- bound: rules never read the clock, so this read says "as of now" and
-- the reader decides what late means. In flight, lost, or refused by
-- the venue all look the same from here until the venue says more.
predicate Unobserved(order: Subject, qty: Decimal)
derived Unobserved(order):
    over (OrderAuthorised(order, _, _, _, _, _)
          and not VenueReport(order_ref: order, ..))
    value qty = value OrderAuthorised(order: order, qty: _, ..)

-- The headline. A report whose reference names no authorised order:
-- the venue accepted an order this record never admitted. Prevention
-- was bypassed somewhere - a second credential, a login used by hand,
-- an executor that invented an order - and the venue's own record is
-- what exposed it. The read names the venue's identifier for the order
-- so that someone can go and look.
predicate Unauthorised(report: Subject, venue_order_id: Subject, qty: Decimal, price: Decimal)
derived Unauthorised(report, venue_order_id):
    over (VenueReport(report: report, order_ref: order_ref, venue_order_id: venue_order_id, ..)
          and not OrderAuthorised(order_ref, _, _, _, _, _))
    value qty = value VenueReport(report: report, qty: _, ..)
    value price = value VenueReport(report: report, price: _, ..)

-- One authorisation, two venue orders. The reports disagree about
-- which external order this is: the venue may have acted twice on one
-- authorisation that reached it twice, or reported one order under
-- two identifiers, or reused a reference. This read says the record
-- holds conflicting observations; it does not say which. `Matched`
-- lists the individual reports for whoever investigates.
predicate Ambiguous(order: Subject, reports: Decimal)
derived Ambiguous(order):
    over (OrderAuthorised(order, _, _, _, _, _)
          and VenueReport(order_ref: order, venue_order_id: first, ..)
          and VenueReport(order_ref: order, venue_order_id: second, ..)
          and first != second)
    value reports = sum(1 | VenueReport(order_ref: order, ..))

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- What this example deliberately does not cover.
--
-- Undoing an unauthorised order: the first rung reports; a cancelling
-- order is an act of its own, later. The venue itself: here it is a
-- fixture in the tests that keeps a ledger of client references and
-- hands out its own order identifiers. Partial fills, amendments and
-- cancellations: a report here is the venue's acceptance of an order,
-- and the lifecycle after that is the trade-lifecycle example's
-- territory. And three things this record asserts about its
-- deployment rather than proves: that the executor alone holds the
-- venue credential, that the reports brought in are all of them, and
-- that the venue echoes client references faithfully. A report nobody
-- brought in is invisible to any read over this record.
